title: Job 3–31 – Job and His Three Friends
description: The Job class, Part 2 of 5: Job's lament, the three cycles of speeches by Eliphaz, Bildad and Zophar with Job's answers, and Job's closing defense, from a chapter-by-chapter study of Job in the King James Bible.
output: lessons/job/job-3-31-job-and-his-friends.html

=== Introduction
--- Job and His Three Friends [title]
overline: The Job class · Part 2 of 5
heading: Job 3–31: Job and His Three Friends
sub: The failure of Job's friends to comfort him in his affliction, rather than accuse him in their hypocrisy.
refs: Job 16:2
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any reference to read the verse.
image: lessons/job/job-3-31-job-and-his-friends.jpg | Shadow-play stage: Job sits on the ash heap with a potsherd while his friends stand over him pointing and accusing

--- Where we are
kicker: The Job class
heading: Part 2: Job and his three friends
tile: 1–2 | The trial begins
tile: 3–31 | **Job and his three friends**
tile: 32–37 | Elihu speaks
tile: 38–41 | Out of the whirlwind
tile: 42 | The latter end
p: So, to recap: Job, devastated by the loss of his children, wealth and health, and wishing he was never born, while yet not sinning against God or cursing Him, sits in ash, scraping his sore-covered flesh, in agony.
p: He had been blessed by these three well meaning friends, who traveled afar, and patiently, in love, sat mourning in silence for a week. [[Job 2:11-13]]

--- The three cycles of speeches
lead: Job 4 will begin a dialogue between Job and the three friends who came to him to mourn.
table: Cycle | Eliphaz | Bildad | Zophar
tr: First | 4–5 · Job 6–7 | 8 · Job 9–10 | 11 · Job 12–14
tr: Second | 15 · Job 16–17 | 18 · Job 19 | 20 · Job 21
tr: Third | 22 · Job 23–24 | 25 · Job 26–31 | —
note: Eliphaz the Temanite, Bildad the Shuhite and Zophar the Naamathite, each answered by Job. Job's lament (chapter 3) opens the dialogue; Job's closing discourses and defense (26–31) end it.

--- Retribution theology
kicker: Job 4
p: His initial argument is made in chapters 4 and 5, and offers a view of “retribution theology,” the idea of karma in God's doings. That sin is the cause of suffering, and that you get what you deserve.
p: Job’s friends defend retribution theology rigorously, and it is difficult to disagree with many of the points made, from a human perspective. Much of it sounds right in our minds. So, before we dive deeper into the text, we should keep in mind the end of the matter, where (spoilers), God addresses the men and what they say.
big: Job 42:7 | mark: ye have not spoken of me the thing that is right, as my servant Job hath
refs: Job 42:7-9

--- Human nature, but not God's
p: This line of thought is just as common today as it was then, and is human nature. But not God's. And while there is general wisdom to be found in their arguments, the application they choose to make is wrong.
callout: As we go, it is good to keep this in mind, especially when we read something that seems perfectly reasonable to us, and may reflect our own views that we may have held against others in our life. It should cause self-reflection.

=== Job's lament
--- Let the day perish
kicker: Job 3
lead: In Job chapter 3, the titular character begins a monologue of absolute dejection and woe.
big: Job 3:3
p: He wishes he was never born. He wishes the day he was conceived would be stricken from time. He asked why he didn't die in the womb rather than suffer as he is. [[Job 3:11]]
p: He wishes for death, that Kings and princes and the wicked and the weary all find rest in, from this life. That the prisoners of death escape the voice of their oppressor and the servant is free from his master. [[Job 3:13-19]]

--- He is miserable
kicker: Job 3
p: He asked why light is given to him that is in misery, why life is given to the bitter in soul, why those who long for death don't receive it.
big: Job 3:20-21
p: He poetically compares the digging for hidden treasure to those that can find the grave.
center: He is miserable.
take: But he has not sinned against God with his lips.
read: app:doctrine-judgment~13685 | Job 3: All babies go to Heaven

=== First cycle
--- The first cycle
kicker: Job 4–14
step: Job 4–5 | **Eliphaz** the Temanite: that Job deserves what has happened to him.
step: Job 6–7 | **Job** answers: did I ask you for anything?
step: Job 8 | **Bildad** the Shuhite: the kids must have deserved it.
step: Job 9–10 | **Job** answers, asking the key question. [[Job 9:2]]
step: Job 11 | **Zophar** the Naamathite: you deserve worse than what you've gotten.
step: Job 12–14 | **Job** answers: he isn't inferior to them in knowledge.

--- Who can withhold himself?
kicker: Job 4 · Eliphaz
p: The first to reply to Job's plaintive cry, cursing the day he was born, is Eliphaz the Temanite, of Teman, a city in Edom. [[Genesis 36:11]] [[Jeremiah 49:7]] [[Ezekiel 25:13]]
p: He starts by asking that, if they attempt to talk with him, will it offend Job? And then immediately, before Job can answer, Eliphaz gives himself permission by saying “Who can withhold themselves from speaking?” [[Job 4:2]]
p: We have all likely, at one time or another been on the receiving or delivering end of this. “I know you're going through a lot, so I don't want to offend you, but I can't help myself...”
p: As well intentioned as this sentiment can be, what follows it rarely is a help to the person hearing it.
take: Better to have remained silent than to do this.

--- Who ever perished, being innocent?
kicker: Job 4 · Eliphaz
p: He begins by pointing out the example Job has been for many, that his faith and counsel have helped those in troubled times. Strengthening the weak. But...it is never good to follow a compliment with a “but,” but now that it has come upon Job, he faints and is troubled. [[Job 4:3-6]]
p: And rather than waiting for a reply to his condescending “tough love,” Eliphaz answers his own question in the least charitable way possible; by accusing Job of hidden sin.
big: Job 4:7
p: This is the crux of his argument, That Job deserves what has happened to him. That he is reaping what he has sown.
callout: “What goes around comes around,” even when true, is the last thing we should say to another when we're trying to comfort them. Especially considering the grace God has extended to us.

--- Stuff happens
kicker: Job 4 · Eliphaz
p: In **Luke 13**, Jesus answers his disciples, who suggest that recent tragedies that happened to others was a direct result of their sin, that it was karma. Jesus says, nay, and that something evil can happen to anyone by chance, but if men don't repent, everyone will likewise perish. Stuff happens. The rain falls on the just and the unjust alike. [[Luke 13:1-5]]
p: A similar question is asked in **John 9:1-3**.
big: John 9:2-3
p: The Truth is we cannot assume by someone suffering that they deserved it for some secret sin that we imagine they did.
take: But we are all deserving of death and hell, and it is by the grace of God that we yet live.

--- True things to make false points
kicker: Job 4 · Eliphaz
p: Eliphaz continues, letting us know of a vision that he had just before sleep, of a spirit he thinks he glimpsed and a voice he heard, that asked:
big: Job 4:17
p: His private interpretation, his own inner musings that he paints as a spiritual vision, posits that since man is obviously not as holy as God, and if even the Angels were punished for their rebellion, how much more man is under judgment for our wickedness. [[Job 4:18-19]] [[2 Peter 2:4]] [[Jude 1:6]]
center: Again, Eliphaz uses true things to make false points.
take: Many times the greatest lies are based on a misrepresentation or misapplication of the greatest truths. [[Ecclesiastes 9:2]]

--- Despise not the chastening
kicker: Job 5 · Eliphaz
p: Eliphaz's monologue continues, directly from chapter 4, and he continues to paint a picture blaming Job for his own suffering. Implying secret sin that has led him to his current condition.
p: He says to call upon the righteous, and see if any man would answer him. Some Catholics use this verse as a proof text for praying to saints. But Eliphaz is not encouraging Job to pray to the dead, but to appeal to the stories of the righteous to find if any ever suffered as he did. [[Job 5:1]]
p: He says that this trouble doesn't come up out of nowhere, but that men are born into trouble and that it comes up like sparks flying out of a fire. [[Job 5:6-7]]
big: Job 5:17
p: So he is quoting scripture, and some of what he is saying is true. But he's misapplying it by assuming that that is the cause of his friend's misery. [[Proverbs 3:11]]

--- Know thou it for thy good
kicker: Job 5 · Eliphaz
panel-warn: Crushed in the gate | And worse, he mentions children being crushed at the gate, knowing that Job's own sons and daughters were killed by a collapsing house. He insensitively talks about having his things spoiled and stolen. [[Job 5:4-5]]
p: It's something we should be aware of because, far too often we judge a matter by its appearance. And we have a ready verse to give. And we pride ourselves in it when we should be comforting the afflicted.
p: But you can tell Eliphaz really thought he was cooking here, giving these great words of wisdom to a man laying in ash, with a shaved head for mourning, scraping the oozing sores on his flesh.
take: Telling him to hear it now and know that it's for thy own good, is the last thing Job needs to hear. [[Job 5:27]]

--- Heavier than the sand of the sea
kicker: Job 6 · Job answers Eliphaz
p: Job begins his reply to Eliphaz's accusations of sin being the cause of Job's woe. Job is overcome by his faithless friend's words.
big: Job 6:2-3
p: That while the poisoned arrows and terrors of God be in him and against him, Eliphaz chose to lecture Job instead of pitying him. [[Job 6:4]]
p: They may be true, but they are obvious truths that Job is already well aware of. They aren't great insights, and they do nothing to help him in his grief. [[Job 6:5-6]]

--- The Holy One
kicker: Job 6 · Job answers Eliphaz
big: Job 6:10 | mark: the words of the Holy One
p: Here, Job gives his confession of faith, his trust in the Holy One, Christ Jesus. He is his strength and his hope. If his end is heaven, why prolong his life?
p: Job’s longing for death is not despair but a plea for release into the only comfort he still trusts: God’s final summons. “Let Him not spare me” is not suicidal rage; it is the confidence that his end is with the Holy One. [[Job 6:8-9]] [[Philippians 1:21-23]]
take: It can be tempting in trials for a believer to long to be called home. But, better to redeem the time while we are here. God's perfect timing is His, not ours.

--- No anachronism
kicker: Job 6 · The Holy One
p: Many argue that Jesus wasn't known in the Old Testament, that salvation by faith didn't exist. That it is an anachronism to see Jesus in Job, and *the Holy One*.
center: There is no anachronism when we see Christ in the Old Testament. It is entirely about Him.
p: He is the Lamb slain before the foundation of the world. The same Word who was with God and was God, Who walked in the furnace with Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego. He was the Rock in the wilderness. [[Revelation 13:8]] [[John 1:1]] [[Daniel 3:25]] [[1 Corinthians 10:4]]
take: The Old Testament saints were saved by the same faith, looking to the same Savior, the same redemption, if through a veil that has now been torn.

--- A deceitful brook
kicker: Job 6 · Job answers Eliphaz
p: And so, Eliphaz's rebuke stings, rather than the comfort of pity, and Job points out this is sin, and turns the accusation back upon Eliphaz, that his erstwhile friend has forsaken the fear of God by his callousness. [[Job 6:14]]
big: Job 6:15
p: That his brother has dealt deceitfully, and gives an allegory of a brook, a small stream that freezes cold in the winter, undrinkable, only to vanish in the heat, dried up. It's a stinging rebuke. [[Job 6:16-20]]
p: Job says, did I ask you for anything? Money? Deliverance? Redemption from captivity? He didn't ask for Eliphaz's opinion either. [[Job 6:22-23]]
p: He says, “look at me,” and asks if he's lying. If there is sin in his speech. Is he saying anything against God? Job's righteousness is in it. It is sorrow that compasses him about. [[Job 6:28-30]]

--- Like the days of an hireling
kicker: Job 7 · Job answers Eliphaz
p: Job continues his defense following Eliphaz's unsympathetic advice. He returns to the theme of chapter 3, where his lamentations for this life are a reflection of the misery he is in.
big: Job 7:1
p: He asks, aren't all men appointed to death? Reminiscent of **Hebrews 9:27**. [[Hebrews 9:27]]
p: Job asks isn't there a time appointed or a limit to the suffering of this life? Can't he earnestly desire the relief of the shadow, shade for the laborer in the Sun, or the reward at the end of his work? [[Job 7:2-4]]
p: We all can relate, especially the older we get, to this wearisome, repetitive life. We go to sleep waiting for the day to come, waking restlessly at the dawn in anticipation for another day of toil.

--- I will not refrain my mouth
kicker: Job 7 · Job answers Eliphaz
big: Job 7:11
p: He's saying I have a right to complain, and has an expectation of sympathy that he hasn't received since opening his mouth.
p: And yet he has not sinned against the Lord.
p: But when he's complaining that he needs rest and he's tired and he's beaten down by all that's happened to him, Eliphaz's “visions” are given to terrify him. To put fear in him that's unfounded, but delivered in a dream. [[Job 7:13-14]]

--- O thou preserver of men
kicker: Job 7 · Job answers Eliphaz
p: And in his anguish, Job turns to the Lord and asks what is man that thou should magnify him. That thou shouldest set thine heart upon him? [[Job 7:17]] [[Psalm 8:4]]
p: But David is writing to glorify God, Job here is not questioning God or sinning against him, But simply asking him why he must continue to live in such misery.
big: Job 7:20
p: He confesses he's a sinner. He asks what shall I do unto the preserver of men, the Lord. He asks for that blessed pardon. [[Job 7:21]]
take: How utterly defeated he is.

--- Bildad the Shuhite
kicker: Job 8 · Bildad
p: And so, following Job's blistering defense against Eliphaz's criticism, and Job's bemoaning his fate, now his friend, Bildad the Shuhite speaks up, in seeming exasperation, asking how long will they have to listen to Job, and insinuating he is full of hot air. [[Job 8:2]]
big: Job 8:4
p: He says the kids must have deserved it, so Job should seek to get right with God with haste, betimes. [[Job 8:5-6]]
panel-warn: Cruel, and wrong | This is cruel, of course, and wrong. He's judging Job's sons and daughters without knowledge, and assuming Job himself is turned from the Lord.

--- The spider's web
kicker: Job 8 · Bildad
p: Bildad preaches the prosperity gospel, that if you're righteous you will be blessed and your life abundant. That God does bless Job that his latter end is better, it is for not cursing God in his tribulation. Not by any acts of righteousness, which also did not save him from this predicament to begin with. [[Job 8:7]]
big: Job 8:13-14
p: He compares this to the backslidden and the hypocrite, whose hope is cut off. Whose trust is as easily destroyed as a spider's web. His house, unstable. Like those who build upon sand in Christ's parable about building upon the rock of His word. [[Matthew 7:24-27]]
take: Again, a holier than thou attitude, given in place of solace and mercy.

--- How should man be just with God?
kicker: Job 9 · Job answers Bildad
p: And here in chapter 9, Job again replies to the admonishment of a friend against him, saying sure, this is true, but then asking the key question;
big: Job 9:2
p: He says, if one were to argue with God, they couldn't win one of a thousand points of contention. That His wisdom and strength is greater than all others. [[Job 9:3-10]]
center: How can any man then be perfect before Him?
refs: Romans 3:10-12; Romans 1:20

--- Because God is just
kicker: Job 9 · Job answers Bildad
p: If God punishes the sinful, and no man is without sin, then any fate, even as extreme as what he's going through, is justified.
center: Because God is just.
p: So what could he say? How could he reason with God as to why he doesn't deserve what he's getting, when his friends all agree that sinners get punished while the righteous prosper, yet there are none righteous!?
big: Job 9:22
p: And so, Job comes to the conclusion that God destroys the perfect and the wicked alike. Bad things happen to good people, and bad people prosper. So it cannot be as Job's friends described. And if it's not, then all the lectures against him are hypocritical and vain.

--- No daysman betwixt us
kicker: Job 9 · Job answers Bildad
p: That even if he washed himself in purest water of melted snow, they would still hold him guilty. [[Job 9:30]]
big: Job 9:33
p: But with God, there is no parity. There's no coming together and reasoning with the Lord, who knows all. There's no mediator of men between them, he says.
p: His faith is in his Redeemer, and his Lord, but the only mediator between man and God, the man Christ Jesus, isn't a man yet. [[1 Timothy 2:5]]
take: That his situation is such, as he seeks to remain faithful and unwilling to curse God in his integrity, he doesn't know what he's supposed to do.

--- Shew me wherefore
kicker: Job 10 · Job answers Bildad
p: Job continues his lament to God. He is weary of life, bitter of soul. He would ask of God to not condemn him for asking, but to explain why. Why create man to be miserable? Why make someone, and despise them?
big: Job 10:2
p: Here, Job isn't speaking against God's power to shape him and use him as He sees fit, even as a vessel fitted unto destruction. He just wants to know why. [[Isaiah 45:9]] [[Romans 9:21]]

--- Life and favour
kicker: Job 10 · Job answers Bildad
big: Job 10:12
p: Job says that God knows he's not wicked, because he is in the Lord's hands. That not only has he made him of flesh and Bones but has granted him life and favor, grace, the preservation of his spirit. Salvation.
p: But he's full of confusion because he can't understand what the point is. Why he is suffering the way he is. [[Job 10:15]]
take: At this point he would welcome the darkness of nothingness rather than continue in the life or the light. He is that miserable. And that confused. [[Job 10:18-22]]

--- Zophar the Naamathite
kicker: Job 11 · Zophar
p: Now the third of Job's friends speaks, Zophar the Naamathite. And he has quite a bit to say along the same lines as Eliphaz and Bildad.
p: He tells a grieving man sitting in ash with his hair plucked and skin covered in boils, who's lost everything, you deserve worse than what you've gotten.
big: Job 11:6 | mark: God exacteth of thee less than thine iniquity deserveth
center: What a blow.
callout: And isn't this kind of dog piling so typical? Especially the holier than thou hypocrisy and fake piety that comes with those that go along with the crowd on moralizing campaigns.

--- The word is nigh thee
kicker: Job 11 · Zophar
p: And again he says something true, that God's ways are not our own and there are aspects to God that we can never understand. So true. [[Job 11:7-9]]
p: But, we can know God's judgments and His word is ever nigh to us. [[Deuteronomy 30:11-15]]
big: Romans 10:8
p: But the hyper spiritual hypocrites will always couch things about God's word as being unknowable. They'll say you can't judge, while they judge unrighteously.
take: And so, Zophar the Naamathite goes on a long-winded explanation about how Job just needs to repent and everything's going to be great. Prosperity gospel time. [[Job 11:13-15]]

--- Not inferior to you
kicker: Job 12 · Job answers Zophar
p: Job responds to Zophar the Naamathite, and his assertions that none can know the ways of God, His secret things.
p: But this was pontificating nonsense. God reveals Himself to man in innumerable ways. In sundry times and diverse manners. All creation testifying, a parable of God. [[Hebrews 1:1]] [[Romans 1:18-20]]
big: Job 12:3
p: He isn't inferior to them in knowledge, and Job says they are not giving him profound arguments, but things everyone knows. Obvious truths and natural man wisdom.
callout: We see it all the time: “Jesus said not to judge!” they'll say, while judging the righteous for preaching the whole word of God. For preaching standards and separation.

--- Ask now the beasts
kicker: Job 12 · Job answers Zophar
p: Job says he's like the upright man that is scoffed at by his neighbor for calling upon God. People mocking faith is a constant in the believer's life. [[Job 12:4]] [[2 Peter 3:3]]
p: People who are comfortable don't want to deal with people in need. They resent them. [[Job 12:5]]
big: Job 12:7-9
p: Every man knows God is real and all creation testifies His wrath and nature and truth.
take: God controls the fates of nations, reveals secrets. He causes the people to wander in the wilderness. To grope in the dark, all to teach us of His wonderful light. [[Job 12:23-25]]

--- Physicians of no value
kicker: Job 13 · Job answers Zophar
p: Job continues his rebuke of the 3 friends-turned accusers. He is lamenting because he wants to reason with God, not men. [[Job 13:3]]
big: Job 13:4-5
big: Proverbs 17:28
callout: When dealing with someone in grief and inconsolably miserable, it is better to sit in silence and love them, than seek to explain away their feelings, and lecture them what to do.

--- Will ye speak wickedly for God?
kicker: Job 13 · Job answers Zophar
big: Job 13:7
p: Job pleads to God, and says they don't have to reply for Him, especially when their entire premise is based on a lie. He lets them know in no uncertain terms that they will have to answer to God for their temerity in speaking for Him, in unrighteous judgment. [[Job 13:10]]
p: Judging the appearance, not the spirit; [[John 7:24]]
p: Many well meaning people, afraid of sitting in silence need to fill the air with words, and those overcome by sympathetic fear, seek to project those fears of their own misdeeds onto the person suffering. Like Job's friends.

--- Though he slay me
kicker: Job 13 · Job answers Zophar
big: Job 13:15
p: We often quote the first half of this verse, to show our faith being absolute, especially in tribulation. But this is Job not only proclaiming his faith, but accepting God's judgement against him for asking, “Why?”
p: He asks of God two things. To lift His hand from against him, to stop the mounting evil that has befallen Job, and to answer Job's question, Why? [[Job 13:20-23]]
take: Job has humbled himself before the Lord, he just wants to know the cause.

--- There is hope of a tree
kicker: Job 14 · Job answers Zophar
p: He again notes the brevity of life, that the mortality of men is like a flower cut down or a fleeting shadow. [[Job 14:1-2]]
big: Job 14:7
p: But, Job says there is hope of a tree. That even if its branches rot and the stock die in the ground while the roots still there, there is hope. A little water can bring it to bud and bring forth boughs again. [[Job 14:8-9]]

--- If a man die, shall he live again?
kicker: Job 14 · Job answers Zophar
big: Job 14:14-15
p: He says that if he dies, shall he live again? But all the days of his appointed time he will wait till his change comes. This is both the change of death from flesh and the change of being resurrected into eternal life.
p: After the bounds of his life are come, God will call and he will answer and God will do the desire of the work of His hands, The promise of putting on incorruption.
take: What sadness he has. What wisdom he has in his sadness.

=== Second cycle
--- The second cycle
kicker: Job 15–21
step: Job 15 | **Eliphaz** speaks again. Offended by Job's mockery of their so-called wisdom.
step: Job 16–17 | **Job** answers: the failure of Job's friends to comfort him. [[Job 16:2]]
step: Job 18 | **Bildad**, furious, unleashes curses against Job.
step: Job 19 | **Job** answers, with a profession that is clear. [[Job 19:25]]
step: Job 20 | **Zophar** joins in, again: a recitation of wisdom.
step: Job 21 | **Job** answers: why do the wicked live?

--- Thine own mouth condemneth thee
kicker: Job 15 · Eliphaz
p: And so, Eliphaz, the first of the men to reproach Job in his sorrow, speaks again. He calls Job unwise and vain, full of hot air. That his talk is unprofitable and worse, accuses Job of attacking God by attacking them.
big: Job 15:6
center: But we already know this isn't true.
p: Eliphaz says that if God doesn't put trust in His saints, in the righteous, how much worse is His wrath upon the unrighteous? And it is a true saying, as far as it goes. [[Job 15:15-16]]
panel-warn: Wicked | But to take the liberty of God, and use it to accuse his friend of being a worse sinner than they are, deserving all that he gets, is wicked.

--- He believeth not that he shall return out of darkness
kicker: Job 15 · Eliphaz
p: The second half of Eliphaz's harangue is interesting, and worth noting. It shows an understanding of salvation and Hell for the unbeliever, even from the mouth of this bad friend. Like other bits of misapplied wisdom in these men's words against Job, we shouldn't throw the baby out with the unsympathetic bathwater.
big: Job 15:22
p: Return out of darkness, the resurrection. The eternal life granted believers. He doesn't believe, and so awaits death as a condemned man waits for the sword.
p: These evil men go up against God, but their strength and bucklers, their defense and their armor, all fail them. While the believer has the strength of the Lord to rely on. [[Job 15:25-26]] [[Psalm 18:2]]

--- The congregation of hypocrites
kicker: Job 15 · Eliphaz
p: Unlike Job's picture of salvation in the tree that dies, even to the roots, but shoots forth new life at the rain, these men of iniquity will fall, be cast off. Fruitless. Vain. [[Job 15:30-33]]
big: Job 15:34-35
take: The congregation of hypocrites. What an accurate description of Hell. Made desolate, no fruit to their account, all they are and have is vanity. Useless. The only thing they produce is mischief and deceit.

--- Miserable comforters
kicker: Job 16 · Job answers Eliphaz
big: Job 16:2
p: And isn't that the whole of the matter, the failure of Job's friends to comfort him in his affliction, rather than accuse him in their hypocrisy.
p: He says that if the roles were reversed, he could speak as they do, shaking his head in judgement, but he wouldn't. Job would try to assuage them. [[Job 16:4-5]]

--- My God, my God
kicker: Job 16 · Job answers Eliphaz
big: Job 16:10-11
p: This is, of course, literally the feeling of Job in his wretched condition, yet he speaks in the Spirit throughout, and it is difficult not to see Jesus' suffering in this entire exchange.
p: But as prophetically accurate, it still is Job's lament: [[Psalm 22:1]]
refs: Psalm 22:6-8; Psalm 22:13; Psalm 22:16-18
read: psalm-22 | Psalm 22

--- My witness is in heaven
kicker: Job 16 · Job answers Eliphaz
big: Job 16:19
p: And in all that, the faith of Job is clear. This is Jesus and His wonderful advocacy for us sinners before the judgment of God. Job is certain of that, despite all else.
big: Job 16:21
take: An indictment of the three, but also an appeal to Christ the mighty counsellor.

--- Poor Job
kicker: Job 17 · Job answers Eliphaz
center: Poor Job.
p: He wants to die, he feels like he is, and he is surrounded by people he thought well of, and who thought well of him, and now they are mockers, surrounding him. [[Job 17:1-2]]
p: He cries to God that He has hidden understanding from these men, who in the past had flattered him as just. But now he is a byword, a cautionary tale in their mouths. [[Job 17:4-6]]
big: Job 17:15
take: I am ready for the grave, he says. To go down into the pit. To be as other men in the dust. What a miserable creature he is, even more so at the turning of his friends.

--- Bildad unleashes curses
kicker: Job 18 · Bildad
p: Bildad, furious at the rebuke of Job against him and the other two fairweather friends, unleashes curses against Job.
p: Bildad says, you dare to accuse us of being wicked? And then lists all the curses that Job has suffered as good. As the wrath of God against Job. [[Job 18:3-13]]
big: Job 18:17
panel-warn: Without mercy | No more is it vague platitudes and misused proverbs, he is directly attacking Job, fiercely and without mercy. Telling this man who was his friend, in grief beyond comprehension, that it is good it happened, he deserves it.

--- Let your light so shine
kicker: Job 18 · Bildad
p: Imagine being the father of 10 goodly adult children, and losing them all at once, and then having the man you thought came to comfort, now berating you.
p: When bad things happen to friends, it is an opportunity given by God to show His love. His light, on the darkness they are in.
big: Matthew 5:16
p: We are to comfort the broken hearted, with the love of God that His glory may be shown from Heaven. Not make it about ourselves.
take: Bildad finishes his cruel screed by accusing Job of being wicked and not knowing God. All to a broken man, laying in dirt made mud by his tears. [[Job 18:21]]

--- Have pity upon me
kicker: Job 19 · Job answers Bildad
p: After the merciless railing by Bildad, Job again offers his defense. He lists in all the ways that God has clearly set against him, and the reproach he has become to those around him. His friends, family, kin, wife. All are opposed to him in his sorry state. [[Job 19:6-20]]
center: Job is destroyed on every side.
big: Job 19:21
p: This is all he asked for from them. Not belaboring the obvious and accusing him relentlessly, rubbing his nose in his plight.

--- I know that my redeemer liveth
kicker: Job 19 · Job answers Bildad
scripture: Job 19:23-27 | mark: For I know that my redeemer liveth
p: We know his words were preserved, forever by the power of God, and his confession of Faith in his living redeemer, his Savior, Who shall stand in the latter day upon the earth. Christ Jesus.
p: The resurrection is his hope and he knows he will see it come. His own eyes will witness the Lord in that day. The same blessed hope we share to this day.
take: If he suffered, be afraid. For the wrath of God is obviously real. They should fear the same recompense for their behavior, and repent. [[Job 19:28-29]]

--- Be not rash with thy mouth
kicker: Job 20 · Zophar
p: Zophar the Naamathite joins in, again, and he says that all this back and forth with Job has triggered in him a need to interject, to speak with haste. He is going to proceed to give a recitation of wisdom, that is all undeniably true. [[Job 20:2-3]]
p: However, he neglected some of God's good advice;
big: Ecclesiastes 5:2
p: Despite the truth Zophar tells, the things he cannot wait to say, his timing and intent, as a reproach to Job and a counter to Job's chastisements of the froward trio, makes him a fool. [[Romans 1:22]]

--- The portion of a wicked man
kicker: Job 20 · Zophar
p: But his words are wise in the right context. God is just to reward the hypocrite and those that work iniquity, even the rich man is punished in their unrighteousness, knowing that whatever they stored up in this life will perish. [[Matthew 6:19-21]] [[Matthew 16:26]]
big: Job 20:29
p: The rich man in Hell, begging for a drop of water, while the poor beggar Lazarus, in similar condition to Job, except his whole life, destitute and covered in sores, is met in Heaven by Abraham, knowing peace for eternity. [[Luke 16:19-24]]
take: But it also reveals his own disregard for the beggar, making his lecture vanity, in hypocrisy.

--- Wherefore do the wicked live?
kicker: Job 21 · Job answers Zophar
p: Job's reply to Zophar is softer than he deserves, and less deliberately scornful, because Job acknowledges the truth behind what he said. He points out that his complaint isn't to men, but to God. [[Job 21:4]]
big: Job 21:7
p: God's wrath isn't present on them always. They are blessed with wealth and safety. But in a moment, it's all lost as they go down into the grave. [[Job 21:13]]
p: Job says they say unto God, depart from us. They, like the **Romans 1** reprobate do not wish to retain God in their knowledge. [[Job 21:14]] [[Romans 1:21]]

--- Reserved to the day of destruction
kicker: Job 21 · Job answers Zophar
big: Job 21:30
p: Job talks about the judgment, reserved for the dead in that day. The great White Throne; [[Revelation 20:11-12]]
p: Job is stipulating that he agrees with the true sayings made by Zophar. He has no quarrel with the obvious truth.
p: But, how is it comforting to tell him all these things he knows, about the God he fears? Because in their answer is the lie that Job is actually wicked.
take: But he is the most faithful man alive.

=== Third cycle
--- The third cycle
kicker: Job 22–25
step: Job 22 | **Eliphaz**, in his final outburst, lays charge to Job.
step: Job 23–24 | **Job** answers: when he is tried, he shall come forth as Gold.
step: Job 25 | **Bildad**'s short response: how can man be justified with God?
step: Job 26–31 | **Job** answers, and comes to the conclusion of his final defense.

--- Is not thy wickedness great?
kicker: Job 22 · Eliphaz
p: Where Zophar the Naamathite kept his argument to generally true sayings, only hinting at Job's supposed guilt being the cause of his suffering, Eliphaz the Temanite, in his final outburst, lays charge to Job.
big: Job 22:5
p: He accuses him of great wickedness and infinite iniquity. Breaking pledges, and the arms of the fatherless. Sent widows away empty handed. He even accuses Job of engaging in the level of wickedness mankind had reached before the flood. [[Job 22:6-9]] [[Job 22:15-16]]
p: It seems as though the railing accusations come from a resentment of Job's well-known perfection in his faith. The righteous living that made God point him out to Satan to begin with. [[Job 1:8]]

--- Sour grapes
kicker: Job 22 · Eliphaz
big: Job 22:3
p: Eliphaz claims that God is not pleased by well doing, and doesn't care if man makes his ways perfect, complete and mature.
center: This seems like sour grapes.
p: We know that God not only doesn't delight in the destruction of the evil, that He would that all men be saved. The Lord takes pleasure in His children's righteousness. [[Psalm 149:4]] [[Psalm 35:27]]

--- The leaven of the Pharisees
kicker: Job 22 · Eliphaz
p: Eliphaz is correct in one thing; the gospel. [[Job 22:22-23]]
p: But he uses this truth as an accusation against Job, presuming he has taken pride in his own righteousness, and not humbled himself as Eliphaz clearly thinks he has.
p: He is a hypocrite, and I believe it stems from feelings of inadequacy and envy when he compares himself to Job.
callout: People who trust in their own works of righteousness live in a sweet spot where they have to be less sinful than the next person. And so they have to resent anyone who upsets this equation by being better than they are.

--- I shall come forth as gold
kicker: Job 23 · Job answers Eliphaz
p: Job again replies to his friends, and makes it clear his thoughts on God, for he is a righteous man. He just wishes he could find God, and defend himself before Him. [[Job 23:3-5]]
p: That God's great power would not be brought against him, but His strength put in him. This is Christ, the advocate. The Righteousness of God. Counsellor. Our deliverer. [[Job 23:6-7]]
big: Job 23:10
p: This is the comfort of all the afflicted who are children of God. Regardless of what tribulation we have on this earth, we are tried as gold, and redeemed.

--- More than my necessary food
kicker: Job 23 · Job answers Eliphaz
big: Job 23:12
p: A reminder that man does not live by bread alone, but by every word of God. [[Matthew 4:4]]
p: But, he wonders, if God has judged him, who can turn Him? How can God repent if it is His will that Job suffer? [[Job 23:13]]
take: Therefore, Job is troubled. This is the crux of his complaints. He fears God and knows Him, and so this affliction troubles him to his soul.

--- Some remove the landmarks
kicker: Job 24 · Job answers Eliphaz
p: Here in chapter 24, Job paints a picture and gives parables of the wicked, and both their iniquity, their seeming prosperity, and their ultimate fate.
big: Job 24:2
callout: There is an allegory here, that can be used to take this and apply it to the false teachers of today. Vipers who “move the landmarks,” the Biblical fundamentals, in order to steal away the flocks of God. To feed on them, spiritually and literally, for filthy lucre's sake.
p: God's truth is eternal, not archaic.

--- They rebel against the light
kicker: Job 24 · Job answers Eliphaz
p: Having just been accused of some of these wicked sins, Job is demonstrating a keener understanding about these matters than even his friends. [[Job 24:3-12]]
big: Job 24:13
p: As he lists them, Job talks about the light and the dark, that the evil hate the light. [[John 3:20]]
take: Job finishes, and asks, who will call him a liar? Do they disagree with him? Has he not shown the understanding they were accusing him of lacking? [[Job 24:25]]

--- How can man be justified with God?
kicker: Job 25 · Bildad
p: Bildad's short response to Job is less accusatory, and more reflective after Job's previous statements on the nature of God, His wrath on the unrighteous, and the hope of salvation.
big: Job 25:4-6
p: His question, with a God so pure and holy, how can man be justified with God? How can any man be clean that is born of women. That men are worms before Him.

--- A little lower than the angels
kicker: Job 25 · Bildad
p: But even in this rhetorical question, God shows us His plan. To be made little lower than the angels. To be born of woman and be pure. To be a son of man, a worm, bearing the reproach of men, and yet Jesus was made hope from the womb. [[Psalm 22:6]] [[Psalm 22:9]]
big: Hebrews 2:9
p: Pierced hands and feet. Garments parted by lot. And still, raised from the dead, declaring the deliverance of the meek, those that fear Him, to all kindreds, the Governor among nations. [[Psalm 22:16-18]] [[Psalm 22:27-28]] [[Hebrews 2:11-12]]

=== Job's closing defense
--- Job's closing discourses
kicker: Job 26–31
step: Job 26 | Job instructing the three men of the nature of God, salvation and creation.
step: Job 27 | The vanity and worthlessness of the treasure built up by the wicked.
step: Job 28 | Fear of the Lord and departing from evil is where understanding is found.
step: Job 29 | His life before the tragedy.
step: Job 30 | Now the byword, the laughing stock of the young men.
step: Job 31 | The conclusion of his final defense.

--- The thunder of his power
kicker: Job 26 · Job answers Bildad
p: Following Bildad's short speech on the Holiness of God compared to man, Job answers. He asks how has he helped others in need? A man may answer of themselves, but Job makes it clear, it is of God. [[Job 26:2-4]]
big: Job 26:14
callout: The dialogue has begun to transition from talking about Job as if he were a heathen, sinful, ignorant and scornful of God and His ways, to Job instructing the three men of the nature of God, salvation and creation, which is a parable offered to all men, plain to see.
p: The stars in the heavens, the earth in space, the oceans and land that bound the waters, all speak of His power and glory. [[Job 26:7-10]] [[Psalm 19:1-3]]

--- The hope of the hypocrite
kicker: Job 27 · Job's parable
big: Job 27:6
p: Job, like the psalmist, has seen the wicked prosper, while he himself is vexed, but despite his bitter circumstances, Job will not reject his righteousness, which is in the strength of the Lord. [[Psalm 73:3]] [[Psalm 73:16-17]]
big: Job 27:8
p: While the widow of a believer can take comfort in their rest when they die, the unbeliever is going to eternal torment, their wealth motheaten and dust. This is the portion of a wicked man. [[Mark 8:36]]

--- The smoke of their torment
kicker: Job 27 · Job's parable
p: In the past week, many have turned out to declare the false doctrine of “annihilationism,” that there is no eternal torment in store for the unbeliever.
big: Revelation 14:11-12
take: But, as the psalmist went to the sanctuary of God, and understood their end, as the saints, we are patient with the tribulation of this world, knowing we have peace in death, and taking comfort in the wrath poured out on the wicked. The smoke of their torment rising forever.

--- The vein of wisdom
kicker: Job 28 · Wisdom
p: Job describes the relative ease of finding precious stones, silver, gold, iron and brass, compared to the wisdom of God. [[Job 28:1-12]] [[Proverbs 8:11]]
big: Colossians 2:3
take: In Christ is all God's wisdom and knowledge. Other creatures and creation is nothing compared to Him. Only in Him is understanding. Jesus is the vein of wisdom, through the fear of God.

--- The mind of Christ
kicker: Job 28 · Wisdom
big: 1 Corinthians 2:16
p: What an awesome thing. To have the mind of Christ. But, our flesh is at enmity with the spirit, and so when we walk in the carnal, we have less access to the wisdom of God.
p: We often use **1 Corinthians 2:14** to say that unbelievers cannot understand scripture, but Paul is writing to the church at Corinth to tell them to stop being carnal. They're arguing over personalities of men, who is of Paul and who is of Apollos, and so they are natural men, and shutting themselves off from that font of wisdom in Jesus Christ. [[1 Corinthians 2:14]] [[1 Corinthians 3:1-4]]

--- The fear of the Lord
kicker: Job 28 · Wisdom
big: Job 28:28
p: Job understands that to even begin to grasp the unsearchable richness of the God, that fear of the Lord and departing from evil is where understanding is found.
refs: Proverbs 9:10; 1 Corinthians 2:7-10; 1 Corinthians 3:18-19

--- Righteousness as a robe
kicker: Job 29 · Job remembers
p: He laments again his current situation, wistfully thinking about his life before the tragedy. The Almighty was with him, Job was blessed with children and prosperity and had the respect of the men at the gate. [[Job 29:2-7]]
p: Why? Job tells us. He delivered the poor and the fatherless. Those that had no man. He blessed the dying and gave to the widow in her need. [[Job 29:12-13]]
big: Job 29:14
p: This should be the goal of all Christian men. To have this mind in them as it was in Christ, to start with the Lord, and to lead others in His wisdom, for His purpose and other's profit.

--- Not a longevity award
kicker: Job 29 · Job remembers
p: Respected men of faith in the church don't put on this gravity like a coat when they get named. It isn't a longevity award. You must be these things before you are ever even considered for leadership. And it should be your guide and goal as a young man. [[1 Timothy 3:8-13]]
p: Sobriety and gravity are what we should strive for, coupled with grace and mercy for those who have no one to deliver them.
p: You gain respect and influence by being a doer of the word, by being a humble servant. Not a prideful boaster.
take: With kindness as the goal. Not lordship.

--- Stand as an example
kicker: Job 29 · Job remembers
p: If you are a Christian man in your middle age, and aren't contributing to the mentoring of young men in your life, especially your church, give an honest assessment between you and God as to why that is, and make a commitment to be.
p: You might never be named a deacon or a church officer. You might not hold a title or be given some kind of imprimatur, but your quiet strength and aged wisdom can still be used mightily by God.
take: We all have a talent to edify. Submit yourself to those with authority, and stand as an example to the young men around you.

--- They that are younger than I
kicker: Job 30 · Job's lament
big: Job 30:1
p: Having established that he was respected and honored for his ways, Job says he is now the byword, the laughing stock of the young men. All from the calamity allowed by God. [[Job 30:9-11]]
p: Young people should show more honor for the elders, and respect for the hoary head should include understanding that bad things that come with age will fall on all of us, God willing.
take: Young Christian men especially should seek to learn from their elders, not mock them.

--- A covenant with mine eyes
kicker: Job 31 · Job's defense
p: Job comes to the conclusion of his final defense. Job now ends his words by again listing what he has tried to do, and what the judgment would be had he not.
big: Job 31:1
p: Job made a vow to his own eyes, to not look at a woman with lust.
p: He says that the inheritance of the wicked is destruction. Punishment. God sees everything, so if he has done anything vain or deceitful, then let the Lord do his worst. [[Job 31:2-8]]

--- When God riseth up
kicker: Job 31 · Job's defense
big: Job 31:14-15
p: That day of judgement, the day of the Lord, is coming for all men. How will we answer for our iniquity?
p: This reminds me of the rich young man who asked Jesus what he must do to be saved, and who claimed he had done all these things from his youth. The difference between him and Job being that Job first acknowledged God. [[Matthew 19:16-22]]
p: He trusted in the Lord and fed the stranger and took care of the poor. Cursing no one. Helping everyone. [[Job 31:24-25]]

--- He just wants to know, why?
kicker: Job 31 · Job's defense
big: Job 31:35-36
p: If they were written, he would wear the book of his sins upon his head as a crown. He would accept the punishment of God and the derision of men.
p: And so in all things Job maintained his fear of God, and spoke not in sin. He maintained his integrity and did not curse God.
take: He just wants to know, why?

--- Pure religion and undefiled
kicker: Job 31 · Job's defense
p: One of the things that we should take away from Job is his attitude towards service. Putting others first.
big: James 1:27
p: When you reach the point where your Christianity isn't about you at all, but about God, glorifying Him in service of others, you are reaching perfection. This should be the goal of all Christians. [[Luke 4:18-19]]
take: Let this mind be in you as it is in Christ.

=== What we have seen
--- What we have seen
point: Retribution theology | And while there is general wisdom to be found in their arguments, the application they choose to make is wrong.
point: True things, false points | Many times the greatest lies are based on a misrepresentation or misapplication of the greatest truths.
point: Miserable comforters | It is better to sit in silence and love them, than seek to explain away their feelings, and lecture them what to do.
point: My redeemer liveth | The resurrection is his hope and he knows he will see it come. The same blessed hope we share to this day.
point: As gold | Regardless of what tribulation we have on this earth, we are tried as gold, and redeemed.
point: His integrity | He maintained his integrity and did not curse God. He just wants to know, why?

--- Read on
kicker: The Job class
heading: Read on
p: In chapter 32 we will meet a young man, Elihu, who we will see is offended by Job's defense, and seeks to have respect given him, the wrong way.
